{"name":"Arava","hex":"#a18d71","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[],"entries":[{"title":"Arava, Estonia","blocks":[{"text":"Arava is a village in Anija Parish, Harju County in northern Estonia. It had a population of 40."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arava%2C_Estonia","wikidata":"Q2444027"},{"title":"Arava Stream","blocks":[{"text":"The Arava Stream is an intermittent stream that flows from the approximate mid-point of the Arava desert in Israel's Southern District northward. It is 89 km long, and in many parts, serves as the actual border between Israel and Jordan. The stream originates on the crest of the Notza Ridge, which forms the watershed divide between the Dead Sea and the Red Sea, and from which all streams in the Arava desert flow either north to the Dead Sea or south to the Red Sea. The stream's prominent tributaries include: Nahal Paran, Nahal Barak, Nahal Tzin, Nahal Tzafit, and Nahal Nekorot. In addition to Israel and Jordan, parts of its drainage basin are in the Sinai Peninsula, Egypt."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Arava_0896.jpg/330px-Arava_0896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arava_Stream","wikidata":"Q12409815"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/The_Dead_Sea_1972-2011_-_NASA_Earth_Observatory.jpg/500px-The_Dead_Sea_1972-2011_-_NASA_Earth_Observatory.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/The_Dead_Sea_1972-2011_-_NASA_Earth_Observatory.jpg/500px-The_Dead_Sea_1972-2011_-_NASA_Earth_Observatory.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"This series of Landsat photos shows the mouth Arvara Stream moving from the old southern shore of the Dead Sea to the southern end of the northern basin. In the two most recent photos, a narrow 